# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nova backend — environment variables
# Copy this file to ".env" in the same folder and fill in real keys.
# Nothing here is committed to git (.env is in .gitignore).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PORT=8787

# Where the frontend dev server runs — used for CORS. Leave as-is for local dev.
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# ── The "brain" — reasoning, deciding which tool to call, AND live web search
# (weather, sports scores/schedules, news — anything current) ─────────────
# Get a free key at https://aistudio.google.com  (Get API Key)
# History: started on Anthropic (Claude Haiku 4.5) → tried Groq to run for
# free, but CONFIRMED BY TESTING (hit this live) its free tier hard-caps
# openai/gpt-oss-120b at 8,000 tokens/MINUTE — Nova's own tool schemas alone
# (35+ real tools across Gmail/Calendar/Canvas/Composio) already request
# ~21,000 tokens before any conversation even starts, so EVERY request
# failed outright, not just under heavy use — a dead end, not a rare edge
# case. Landed on Gemini instead, via Google's OpenAI-compatible endpoint
# (same tool-calling shape, see llm.ts) — genuinely cheap without that same
# tight per-minute wall. gemini-2.5-flash-lite is the cheapest model on
# paper ($0.10/$0.40 per million tokens) but CONFIRMED BY TESTING it 404s
# for new API keys — Google's own error names gemini-3.5-flash-lite as the
# replacement ($0.30/$2.50 per million tokens — still cheap in absolute terms).
GEMINI_API_KEY=
GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite

# ── Composio — connects Nova to Gmail, Google Calendar, and anything else in
# their tool catalog ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CONFIRMED BY TESTING (curl against backend.composio.dev/api/v3 directly,
# Sept 2026): Composio has TWO differently-prefixed keys that are easy to
# grab the wrong one of — get this wrong and every request fails with a
# generic "Invalid API key" that doesn't say WHY:
#   - ck_... = a "Connect consumer key" (Connect dashboard → Settings →
#     Sessions & API Key) — for MCP consumer sessions, NOT this.
#   - ak_... = the "Project API key" (Developer dashboard → Project Settings
#     → API Keys) — THIS is what belongs here.
# No toolkit allowlist needed here — whatever you connect through the
# Connectors page (search covers Composio's whole ~1,500-toolkit catalog) is
# automatically usable, no config change required.
# NOTE: GrubHub has no public consumer API, so it isn't a real Composio
# toolkit — the "call a restaurant" flow in this demo is intentionally
# simulated (see backend/src/tools/restaurant.ts) rather than wired to a
# real integration.
COMPOSIO_API_KEY=

# ── Nova's ears and mouth — GPT-Live-1, OpenAI's full-duplex voice model ───
# Get a key at https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
# This is the ONLY thing that listens and speaks — there's no fallback voice
# pipeline anymore, so Nova can't hear or talk without this being set. Nova's
# brain stays Claude/Composio via "client delegation" (see
# backend/src/services/liveDelegate.ts) — this only carries audio.
# Billed at $0.05/min of open voice session, per second, separate from
# Claude/Composio usage — nothing runs (or bills) while she's just waiting
# for the wake word.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_LIVE_MODEL=gpt-live-1

# ── Timezone Nova uses to resolve "this Thursday at 4" etc. ────────────────
# Leave blank to use the server machine's own timezone automatically, or set
# an IANA name, e.g. TIMEZONE=America/Chicago
TIMEZONE=

# ── Twilio — gives every Nova account a phone number they can text (and,
# later, call). Chosen over Inkbox: same idea (an "identity layer" for an
# agent), but Twilio is the established, cheaper, purpose-built platform. ──
# Get these at https://console.twilio.com:
#   - TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID / TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN — Account dashboard home page.
#   - TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER — buy an SMS-capable number (Phone Numbers → Buy a
#     number), in E.164 form, e.g. +15551234567. This is the ONE shared
#     number every user texts — smsDelegate.ts routes by the sender's own
#     number (the `From` field), not by which number they texted.
# Then, on that number's configuration page, set "A message comes in" to
# {PUBLIC_BASE_URL}/api/sms/webhook (HTTP POST).
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_PHONE_NUMBER=

# The public URL Twilio's webhook can reach this server at. Twilio can't POST
# to localhost directly — for local dev, run `ngrok http 8787` and put its
# https://...ngrok-free.app URL here (update it and the Twilio console's
# webhook URL every time ngrok gives you a new one, unless you're on a paid
# ngrok plan with a fixed domain). In production this is your real domain.
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=
